 ษออออออออออออออหอออออออหออออออหอออออออออออออหอออออหอออออหออออออหออออออหอออออป
 บ     p10      บ  p11  บ  p12 บ    p13      บ p14 บ p15 บ p16  บ p17  บ p18 บ
 บ2nd beam groupบlft.posบrt.posบ3rd beam grp.บ lft.บ rt. บ#disp.บthick.บ sizeบ
 ศออออออออออออออสอออออออสออออออสอออออออออออออสอออออสอออออสออออออสออออออสอออออผ


*
                                                                            2/7
   P7 = implied stem direction and number of beams.

          P7 is always a two-digit number.  The left digit indicates the
          stem direction of the notes attached to the beam.

          1 = stems up, 2 = stems down.  The right digit holds the number
          of full beams drawn between position P3 and position P6.

                    P7 = 12 = stems up, 2 full beams
                       = 23 = stems down, 3 full beams

                    (See below re. P7 and tremolo beams.)

   P8 = number to be centered above or below the full beams. (-P8 splits beam)
         Use P16 for % of horiz. displacement.  P16 = 0 = default .5 (50%).
         ******* P19 can shift the number up or down. *******

     When EDITING ONLY: A negative value in P8 is used to split beams into
     smaller units. E.g. if 6 notes are found under a continuous double beam,
     typing '8 -3' will cause the appearance of 1 long single beam and 2
     secondary beams attaching 3 notes each.  The negative number indicates
     the last note to be included under the 1st secondary beam.  In this case
     the number may have 1 or 2 digits.
*
                                                                            3/7
  P8 continued

     If P8 has 3 or 4 digits, the ORIGINAL beam will have 2 partials and a
     NEW beam will be created made up of 1 or 2 partials.  In this case the
     sum of the digits MUST equal the total of the notes involved.
       If there are 9 notes under a beam, P8 = -333 creates 3 partials, 1 for
       each 3 notes.
       If there are 10 notes under a beam, P8 = -3223 creates 4 partials,
       encompassing 3 notes, 2 notes, 2 notes, and 3 notes.

     To use the split beam feature when the beamed group involves notes with
     stems both up and down, an "x" must follow the command. Some further
     editing mahy be necessary.

        Example: 8 -222 x

     After this feature is used P8 will reset itself to 0.



   P9 is used to adjust beam's end points when note stems are not all in
   same direction. (see below)

*
                                                                            4/7
P10, 11, 12 -- P13, 14, 15
Composite partial beams:  2 levels of composite beams may be added to the full
         beams.  Parameters 10, 11, 12 are used for the 1st composite, and
         P13, 14, 15 for the 2nd composite.  The role of the parameters in
         each group of 3 is exactly the same, so only P10, 11, 12 will be
         described.   (See below re. P14, P15 and offset notes.)

    There are 2 kinds of partial beams; attached and unattached.  Attached
    partial beams are secondary beams which do not extend the length of the
    full beam but are attached on both left and right sides to note stems.
    An unattached partial is connected on one side only, leaving the other
    end of the beam free floating.

    P10 is a 2-digit number where the left digit gives the displacement (in
    beam widths) from the level of the full beam(s).  The right digit tells
    how many partial beams will be drawn. If p10 is negative then the added
    beam(s) will appear above the main beam if stems are up, or below the
    main beam if stems are down.  (In this case the displacement value,
    i.e. the left hand digit, will equal the number of partial beams, i.e.
    the right hand digit.  This feature will often be used in situations
    where stems do not all go the same direction.)


*
                                                                            5/7
    P11 sets the left position of the partial beam.  If P11 = -1 the left
    position will equal the value found in P3.

    P12 sets the right position of the partial beam.  If P12 = -1 the right
    position will equal the value found in P6.

    P9 is used in complex situations where some of the note stems do not go
    in the same direction (e.g. notes on staff above or staff below).  P9 may
    have up to 3 digits, (using 0, 1, 2, 3 only).  Here these digits will be
    labeled "abc".  Digit "a" refers to P3 and P6 (left and right ends of the
    main beam).  Digit "b" refers to P11 and P12 (left and right ends of first
    added partial beam).  Digit "c" refers to P14 and P15 (left and right ends
    of second partial beam).  The role of these digits is as follows:
    0=no offset, 1=left beam end is offset, 2=right end offset, 3=both offset

    **** When editing, set P9 to -1 to create the proper values automatically.
    
    This use of P9 insures that the beam end points will exactly meet the note
    stems.

       Be sure to use the centering (CN) and adjust (AD) commands to make sure
       that the beam ends and stem lengths are accurately placed.

*
                                                                            6/7
Tremolos:  P7 = -10 (stems up) or -20 (stems down).
           P10 = "mn" where m = displacement, n = number of tremolo beams.
             If the tremolo beams are not appearing as secondary beams in
             conjunction with full beams then P5 = 0  and P6 = 0.

           P12 can set the width of the tremolo beams. 
             If P12 = 0 the default width is 4.

        P11:  When tremolo beams are used in conjunction with full beams, a
        separate item must be set up with parameters 1 through 6 identical to
        those of the full beam item.  This is necessary so that the tremolo
        beams will be parallel to the slope of the full beam.  Now P11 is used
        to set the particular position of each tremolo unit.

    "Floating" tremolo beams:  P7 = 10 or 20.  The beams will be suspended
     between positions in P3 and P6.  P10 = the number of beams.
     P11 and P12 set distance of beam ends from note stems.
         Default values are 3.5 and -3.5 if P11 = 0 and P12 = 0.

                   (Tremolos continued - )



*
                                                                            7/7
Tremolos with combined attached and floating beams:

 IF: 2nd digit (n) of P7 is NOT = 0 and P10 is NOT = 0 and P11 = 0 and P12 = 0,
THEN: n = number of full-length beams, and P10 will have a 2-digit number (xy),
      where x = offset of floating beams, y = number of floating beams.


P17 = beam thickness factor. (0 = default, .9 = thinner, 1.1 = thicker)
                             (This feature should rarely be used.)

P18 = beam size factor for beams attached to notes with P15 size factor set.
                                                                       
 ******* Beam offsets to match note offsets *******

 ******* P14 and P15 can be used to aid aligning beams with notes which have
an offset value set in the note's P10.   The beam's P13 MUST be left at 0.
The beam's P14 should have the same value as the P10 of the 1st note under 
the beam.  The beam's P15 should have the same value as the P10 of the last 
note under the beam.  Use CN (center) for final alignment.
(Since P13 must = 0, doubly composite beams cannot use this feature.  
Such composites must be broken up into 2 separate items.)

